# freeware/ is the public-domain disk image shipped with os9exec.
# h5 is a symlink -> freeware (repo-root convention post h0/h1 reorg).
# freeware/ content is gitignored until proprietary items are fully removed.
freeware
# h? not h*: the disk mounts are all two characters (h0 h1 h4 he, test/h8..hd).
# h* also matched any FILE starting with h -- and on a case-insensitive
# filesystem, with H too, so e.g. a HANDOFF.md silently refused to be added.
h?
dd

# os9defs/ headers are OURS: reconstructed from books and documentation found
# online, using the information rather than any Microware source. Copyrighted
# facts, not stolen code -- so they are committed. (They are also second-hand
# and not guaranteed Microware-compatible; each header says so at the top.)
# The proprietary Microware headers that once lived here are gone, so there is
# nothing left in that directory to exclude.

# Adapted OS-9 headers (user-derived from originals; not redistributable pending permission)
/Source/OS9exec_core/os9defs/orig/

# Build output
/Output/
/build/
/.build/
/os9exec
/os9exec.exe
/dist/
/test/.build/

# Reference PDFs and research material (large, not redistributable)
/resources/

# Claude Code per-user local settings (machine-specific allowlist)
/.claude/settings.local.json

# Internal planning docs — not ready for public visibility yet
# (index + the per-architecture splits ROADMAP-68k.md / ROADMAP-6809.md)
/ROADMAP*.md
# The companion record of what we decided NOT to build, and why. The roadmaps
# are TODO lists only; a declined or deferred item moves here so it stops
# reading as work-in-hand. Same visibility as the roadmaps: local, not shared.
/DECISIONS*.md

# Session-to-session handoff/continuity notes — for the pair, not the community
*HANDOFF.md
*NEXT-SESSION.md

# Subagent-driven-development scratch state (progress ledger, review packages)
/.superpowers/

# CONF68K run artefacts: the tests' own scratch files (one reaches 70K by
# design -- t07 checks that a write past a 32-bit seek extends a file) and
# the report a run appends to. The suite's recorded results live in
# test/68k-conformance/DOCS/expected, which IS committed.
/test/68k-conformance/SCRATCH/*
!/test/68k-conformance/SCRATCH/readme
/test/68k-conformance/RESULTS/report
/test/68k-conformance/REBUILT/*
!/test/68k-conformance/REBUILT/readme

# Built disk images are release ARTIFACTS, not sources: the tree in freeware/
# is what gets maintained, and release/dd is generated from it by
# release/mkimage.sh. A 182 MB binary in git means no diffs and a full copy per
# revision. Build it, don't version it.
/release/dd
/release/*.rbf

# Working material, not product. These are our notes, process and tooling; they
# are kept locally but not published -- the repo goes to GitHub and to the
# upstream SourceForge project, and nobody downloading os9exec needs our
# scaffolding. The docs/superpowers cards in particular are reading notes taken
# from copyrighted OS-9 manuals (the Guru, Insights, Microware TRMs), which are
# not GPL and are not ours to redistribute.
.claude/
docs/superpowers/
tools/audit-patterns.py

# test/68k-utils/ ships SOURCES only. Its compiled modules cannot be committed:
# they are built with cc -I against the shared `cio` trap handler, which keeps them
# small (load is ~4K rather than 16848), but the vendor's cstart and C library are
# linked in regardless -- not ours to redistribute, whoever's disk they came from.
# (A build against $OS9SDK is at least free of the licensee watermark; see
# test/68k-utils/readme.) cio itself is borrowed from a system disk for a test run
# and removed again, never kept here. Build them on demand with
# tools/68k-utils/build.sh. This is the same rule the rest of the tree follows:
# test/68k-live-verification ships .a and .c only, and CONF68K's CMDS can ship
# precisely because every byte of those modules is hand-written assembly of ours.
/test/68k-utils/CMDS/*
!/test/68k-utils/CMDS/readme
/test/68k-utils/SCRATCH/*
!/test/68k-utils/SCRATCH/readme
# ...and the copy tools/conformance.sh installs into the suite so t49 has a
# `load` to fork. Every leg removes it on the way out; this is the belt for the
# run that was interrupted before it could.
/test/68k-conformance/CMDS/load
/test/68k-conformance/CMDS/cio
